{"files": {"SKILL.md": "# Practical Engineering Discipline\n\nYou receive a problem statement and a working environment. Produce the deliverable the problem names, where it names, in the form it names. Read the problem fully before acting; verify your solution against the problem's own check before stopping.\n\n## Read the contract before building\n\nThe problem document tells you three things: what artifact to produce, where to produce it, and the check that confirms it. Extract those three before any tool call. Re-read any test or verification file the problem references \u2014 its assertions are the literal contract; satisfying every assertion is the success condition. Whatever names, paths, formats, types, and exact strings the problem quotes are constraints, not suggestions.\n\n## Start with a placeholder at the named path\n\nOnce the path is clear, write a minimal version of the deliverable there before deep analysis. A stub of the right shape (empty file with the right header, a function with the named signature returning a default, an empty container of the right type) costs little and locks in \"the file exists at the expected location.\" Replace the stub with real content as understanding grows.\n\nIf two turns pass without the deliverable existing on disk, stop investigating and write a stub first.\n\n## Work from observed evidence, not from inference\n\nWhen the deliverable contains specific values (records, secrets, configuration parameters), source those values from direct reads of the input \u2014 open the file, run the binary, inspect the bytes. Plausible-looking values that come from pattern guessing rather than observation tend to fail strict equality checks. If a value cannot be recovered from the source, leave it blank or note its absence rather than inventing it.\n\n## Match observed shape character-by-character\n\nCopy quoted identifiers (function names, class names, field names, paths) exactly as the problem writes them. Punctuation, capitalisation, separators, and surrounding syntax are part of the contract. A signature like `def name(...)` defines the importable name as `name` \u2014 the `def` and the parameter list are how the language declares it, not part of the name. A consumer that does `from module import name` expects exactly that.\n\n## Iterate against the actual check\n\nAfter every substantive change, invoke whatever the problem names as its check \u2014 a test runner, a configuration validator, a compile-and-run command, a domain-specific tool. Read the full output. Map each failure to its specific cause from the error message itself:\n\n- \"not allowed here,\" \"unknown setting\" \u2192 wrong scope or unsupported option\n- \"cannot import name,\" \"no such attribute\" \u2192 name mismatch or missing export\n- \"wrong count,\" \"value out of range\" \u2192 off-by-one or boundary condition\n- \"no such file,\" \"permission denied\" \u2192 wrong path or missing permission\n- timeout \u2192 blocked call or unbounded loop\n\nAddress the named failure. Surface-level retries reproduce the same failure.\n\n## Task families\n\nDifferent task shapes need different opening moves; the discipline is the same.\n\n### Bug fixes in existing code\n\nThe repository is the spec. Reproduce the failing case first \u2014 run it as the problem describes. Read the function that raises and the function that calls it. The minimal fix is the smallest change that makes the failing case behave correctly without breaking any other case. When the problem asks for the fix as a patch (a unified diff against the source tree), produce the diff with the standard tools so paths and context lines match the working tree exactly. Verify with a parametrized run that both the previously-passing and previously-failing inputs now succeed before declaring done.\n\n### Reproduction by algorithm to match a target\n\nWhen the deliverable reproduces a target by algorithm, read the target bytes first. Their structure reveals the generating function \u2014 uniform repetition implies a constant, smooth axis-aligned progression implies linear interpolation, periodic patterns imply trigonometric functions, smooth transitions with sharp object boundaries imply rendered scenes. Implement that structure directly. Measure your output against the target with the same metric the problem names; iterate parameters until the gap closes. Algorithms must be self-contained \u2014 do not embed lookup tables.\n\n### Recovery from a damaged or obfuscated source\n\nPreserve the source before any tool touches it: copy it aside first, never modify in place until you understand it (open utilities can consume or truncate the source even on read-only operations). Inspect raw bytes (`file`, `xxd`, `hexdump -C`, `strings`) before trusting the format. Try the ecosystem's format-aware recovery tool before writing a parser. When the data is transformed (encoded with a recoverable scheme, not destroyed), find known plaintext to derive the transform from a small fragment, then apply it across the rest. Write the transformed result back at the original path so the system that consumes it can replay its own pending operations on the recovered file; manually rebuilding from base records and visible operations loses any in-flight modifications the original transformation log was holding.\n\n### Configuration and services\n\nInstall packages explicitly. Write complete configuration files; small in-place edits are fragile when values contain dollar-sign variables, embedded quotes, or backslashes \u2014 shell substitution can mangle them silently. Configuration languages partition directives into block-level scopes, with each directive only valid in specific blocks; the docs name the scope per directive. Putting one in the wrong block produces a parse error that names the directive but cascades downstream. Test with the service's own validator before declaring done.\n\nFor setups that combine multiple daemons (a network repository service plus an HTTP server, for example), the script must install, configure, and explicitly start each daemon (no init system runs by default). Create system users with the exact shell, home, and password the verifier authenticates with. Make hook scripts executable. Confirm each daemon is bound to its expected port and that the end-to-end path the verifier will exercise actually works from inside the same script before exit. When directives take quoted string values (log formats, command lines), keep each value on one line, terminated properly, and wrap dynamic placeholders in literal double quotes so consumers that match quoted patterns see them.\n\n### Asynchronous control flow\n\nTrack the work units you create \u2014 keep your own references; the runtime's introspection of \"all currently active work\" includes its own bookkeeping you do not own. When the contract specifies cancellation behavior, the cancel path is its own test: signal each unit you own, drain them, and confirm cleanup blocks actually ran. The path where units are held back behind a concurrency limiter is exactly where naive implementations silently leak \u2014 units that have not started when the cancel arrives need their own signal too.\n\n### Defeating a filter that screens user input\n\nAny filter is one parser; the consumer is another. Where the two disagree, opportunity. Pattern-matching filters skim characters as a single text stream and treat delimited data regions as opaque; structural parsers re-tokenize the same text into elements, sometimes using different tokenization rules in different contexts (an embedded vector-graphics element or mathematical-markup element may switch the parser into an XML-style mode where delimited data regions and tag-boundary rules differ from the outer body). The contract usually specifies the trigger condition \u2014 most often without user interaction \u2014 confirm the candidate fires on parse alone (link-clicks, form submits, hover events, JS-URL navigations are interaction-required and do not satisfy \"automatic\" requirements). When the obvious vectors are blocked, look at the boundaries between parsing modes; the same character sequence reads as data on one side and as structure on the other.\n\n## Write files with the tool that ships bytes verbatim\n\nThe file-write tool preserves the bytes you write. Shell heredocs and `echo` chains can transform metacharacters in surprising ways \u2014 quote handling, variable expansion, escape sequences \u2014 that silently corrupt code, regex, and config containing such characters.\n\n## Before exit\n\nConfirm the artifact exists at the exact named path, is non-empty, and is structurally valid for its type. Run the named check once more and confirm it passes. If full correctness is out of reach, leave the best partial artifact in place at the correct path \u2014 present-but-imperfect outscores missing.\n\n## Refuse to fake\n\nDon't invent data the problem didn't supply. Don't mark a test passed without running it. Don't modify the check, the test files, or the problem statement to engineer success. A modest, honest result beats a constructed one.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}